0584","person_slug":"rachel-siewert","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Richard Colbeck","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10131","person_slug":"richard-colbeck","vote":"no"},{"name":"Richard Di Natale","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10755","person_slug":"richard-di-natale","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Ron Boswell","party":"Nationals","person_id":"tvfy_10057","person_slug":"ron-boswell","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sarah Hanson-Young","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10711","person_slug":"sarah-hanson-young","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Scott Ludlam","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10713","person_slug":"scott-ludlam","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Scott Ryan","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10715","person_slug":"scott-ryan","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sean Edwards","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10756","person_slug":"sean-edwards","vote":"no"},{"name":"Simon Birmingham","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10044","person_slug":"simon-birmingham","vote":"no"},{"name":"Stephen Conroy","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10142","person_slug":"stephen-conroy","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Stephen Parry","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10507","person_slug":"stephen-parry","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sue Boyce","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10062","person_slug":"sue-boyce","vote":"no"},{"name":"Trish Crossin","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10161","person_slug":"trish-crossin","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Ursula Stephens","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10609","person_slug":"ursula-stephens","vote":"aye"}],"name":"Committees — Treaties Committee; Meeting","noes":30,"question":"Anne McEwen by leave—On behalf of the Joint Standing Committee on Treaties, I move: That the Joint Standing Committee on Treaties be authorised to hold a public meeting during the sitting of the Senate on Monday, 31 October 2011, from 10 am to 1.30 pm. Helen Kroger Mr President, I seek leave to make a statement. John Hogg Leave is granted for two minutes. Helen Kroger I want to explain why we are opposing this. It is the government that has changed the hours that the Senate is sitting. It is the government itself that has extended those sitting hours. It has included another week in November. It has done that in collusion with the Greens so that we can deal with the toxic carbon tax. It has provided for and allowed only 20 hours of debating time. That is time in which we believe that, in the interests of all Australians, we should be in this chamber and be giving due consideration, discussion, dialogue and scrutiny to it. We do not believe holding public hearings during the time that the carbon tax is being considered in this chamber to be in the interests of those who elected us to this place. So this is no reflection on our consideration of the importance of this public hearing, but we do believe if the government cannot get its act together and organise its business hours appropriately to allow for public hearings outside parliamentary sitting times then that is something that we should not support. We do not support it and the Greens should not support it. It is an indictment upon them that the Deputy Chair of the Joint Standing Committee on Treaties himself, Senator Simon Birmingham, will not be able to attend the public hearing because as the shadow parliamentary secretary for the environment he will be in this place. He will not be able to attend the hearing even though he is deputy chair. On that basis, we oppose this motion. Question put: That the motion (Senator McEwen's) be agreed to. The Senate divided. [12:43] (The President—Senator Hogg) _Senator Feene","result":"affirmative","slug":"division-federal-senate-2113","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2011-10-13/3"}
